## The work — a checklist over the existing folder

### ➕ Add — `boukensha/client.py`

The only substantial new code. Constants first, mirroring Ruby exactly:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `RETRYABLE_STATUS_CODES = [408, 409, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504]` | same list (a tuple or frozenset is fine — it is only ever membership-tested) |
| `MAX_RETRIES = 3` | same |
| `BASE_RETRY_DELAY = 0.5` | same |
| `retry_delay(attempt) = BASE_RETRY_DELAY * (2**(attempt - 1))` | same — yields 0.5, 1.0, 2.0 |

`TRANSIENT_ERRORS` needs a real mapping, not a transliteration:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `Errno::ECONNRESET` | `ConnectionResetError` |
| `Errno::ECONNREFUSED` | `ConnectionRefusedError` |
| `Net::OpenTimeout`, `Net::ReadTimeout`, `Timeout::Error` | `TimeoutError` (`socket.timeout` is an alias for it in 3.10+) |
| `OpenSSL::SSL::SSLError` | `ssl.SSLError` |
| `SocketError` | `socket.gaierror` (DNS failure) |
| `EOFError` | `EOFError` |
| — | `urllib.error.URLError` — the wrapper `urllib` puts around most of the above |

> ⚠️ **`urllib.error.HTTPError` is a subclass of `URLError`. Catch it first.**
> This is the one structural trap in the step. Ruby's `Net::HTTP#request`
> *returns* a response object whatever the status, so Ruby checks
> `response.code` afterwards. `urllib.request.urlopen` instead **raises**
> `HTTPError` on any non-2xx. If the `except` clauses are ordered the other way
> round, every 500 is swallowed by the transient-error branch and the
> status-code retry list never runs — the failure is silent and looks like a
> flaky network.

Structure of `call`:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `def initialize(builder)` | `def __init__(self, builder): self.builder = builder` |
| `def call(max_output_tokens: 1024)` | `def call(self, *, max_output_tokens=1024)` |
| `URI(@builder.url)` + `Net::HTTP.new` + `use_ssl` | `urllib.request.Request(self.builder.url(), data=…, headers=self.builder.headers(), method="POST")` — `urlopen` picks TLS from the scheme, so there is no `use_ssl` line to port |
| `request.body = payload.to_json` | `json.dumps(payload).encode()` — `urlopen` needs bytes |
| `loop do … attempts += 1` | `while True:` with the same counter |
| `rescue *TRANSIENT_ERRORS => e` | `except TRANSIENT_ERRORS as e:` (a tuple) |
| `raise ApiError, "…" if attempts > MAX_RETRIES` | same guard, same message |
| `sleep retry_delay(attempts); next` | `time.sleep(self._retry_delay(attempts)); continue` |
| `retryable_response?(response) && attempts <= MAX_RETRIES` | `status in RETRYABLE_STATUS_CODES and attempts <= MAX_RETRIES` |
| `JSON.parse(response.body)` | `json.loads(body)` |

Message strings, to the byte:

- transient exhaustion —
  `f"API request failed after {attempts} attempts: {type(e).__name__}: {e}"`
  (Ruby's `e.class` prints `Errno::ECONNRESET`, Python's prints
  `ConnectionResetError`; the shape matches, the class names cannot)
- non-success —
  `f"API request failed after {attempts} attempt{'' if attempts == 1 else 's'} ({status}): {body}"`

Note Ruby pluralizes only the second message; the first always says "attempts"
even when there was one. Mirror the inconsistency rather than tidying it.

The retry ceiling is **4 total attempts** (1 initial + `MAX_RETRIES`). Check the
port against that number, since the `>` / `<=` asymmetry between Ruby's two
guards makes it easy to land on 3 or 5.

> **`verify_mode` and the certificate comment do not port.** Ruby sets
> `OpenSSL::SSL::VERIFY_PEER` and carries a commented-out `ca_file` line with a
> note about macOS vs Linux/WSL2 cert paths; the README devotes a section to it.
> Python's `urlopen` verifies against the system trust store by default, so the
> whole problem is absent. Do not construct an `ssl.SSLContext` to have
> something to point at. Note the disappearance in the README, the same way
> step 02 noted `transform_keys`.

Ruby passes no explicit timeouts, taking `Net::HTTP`'s 60s open/read defaults;
`urlopen` with no `timeout` blocks on the global socket default (usually
forever). See **OQ 3**.

